{
  "version": 1,
  "slug": "jeva-fresh-decisions",
  "chapters": [
    {
      "number": 1,
      "title": "Fixed options, changing judgments",
      "claims": [
        {
          "id": "c1-beat-1",
          "claim": "Jeva makes a decision by changing its view of a request while keeping the available options fixed.",
          "caption": 0,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Independent encoding and mutable state",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "README.md",
              "lines": "17-22"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-beat-2",
          "claim": "The same language encoder reads the request and each option separately. An option sees its type and text, but no current request.",
          "caption": 1,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Typed input templates",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/_runtime/collate.py",
              "lines": "4-9"
            },
            {
              "label": "Separate state and option encoding",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/_runtime/model.py",
              "lines": "38-48"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-beat-3",
          "claim": "The resulting option vectors become a memory. Each decision block projects that memory into keys and values without rewriting the options.",
          "caption": 2,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Prepared normalized option memory",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/_runtime/matrix_decision.py",
              "lines": "247-275"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-beat-4",
          "claim": "The request supplies a query. Similarity weights the option values, and their weighted sum nudges the request state.",
          "caption": 3,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Weighted value aggregation and state residual updates",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/_runtime/matrix_decision.py",
              "lines": "124-159"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-beat-5",
          "claim": "Here is the paper\u2019s exact two dimensional example, not a measured model output. Two options initially tie against the same request.",
          "caption": 4,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Jeva paper v1, Section 3.2, Proposition 1",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2610.18773v1"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-beat-6",
          "claim": "A third option above the axis pulls the refined state upward. The upper option now wins, even though neither original option moved.",
          "caption": 5,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Jeva paper v1, Section 3.2, Proposition 1",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2610.18773v1"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-beat-7",
          "claim": "Replace only the third option with its reflection. The state moves downward, and the lower option wins instead.",
          "caption": 6,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Jeva paper v1, Section 3.2, Proposition 1",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2610.18773v1"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-beat-8",
          "claim": "That reversal comes from reading the whole set. Independently scoring each retained option cannot reverse their relative preference this way.",
          "caption": 7,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Jeva paper v1, Sections 3.1 and 3.2, equations 2 and 5",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2610.18773v1"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-beat-9",
          "claim": "Now reorder the same options. Their weights move with them, so the weighted sum and the refined state stay the same.",
          "caption": 8,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Permutation-equivariant attention and invariant weighted sum",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/_runtime/matrix_decision.py",
              "lines": "137-159"
            },
            {
              "label": "Jeva paper v1, Section 3.4, Proposition 3",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2610.18773v1"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-beat-10",
          "claim": "Probabilities follow option identity, and a unique winner survives reordering. Exact ties use input order; floating point arithmetic can perturb near ties.",
          "caption": 9,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "First maximum and open-set tie handling",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/inference.py",
              "lines": "255-268"
            },
            {
              "label": "Finite precision caveat",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "docs/runtime.md",
              "lines": "83-85"
            },
            {
              "label": "Jeva paper v1, Appendix B.5",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2610.18773v1"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-beat-11",
          "claim": "The options are reusable, but the judgment remains sensitive to the available set. Next, we must ask whether any option belongs at all.",
          "caption": 10,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Set dependence and null awareness",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "README.md",
              "lines": "17-29"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "number": 2,
      "title": "Which answer, or no answer?",
      "claims": [
        {
          "id": "c2-beat-1",
          "claim": "A favorite among poor options can still be a poor answer. Jeva separates competition between candidates from coverage of the request.",
          "caption": 0,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Competition and coverage are separate",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/_runtime/matrix_decision.py",
              "lines": "391-404"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c2-beat-2",
          "claim": "Competition turns score differences into relative probabilities. In this illustrative example, the first option gets about seventy three percent.",
          "caption": 1,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Conditional score softmax",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/_runtime/matrix_decision.py",
              "lines": "391-397"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c2-beat-3",
          "claim": "Lower both scores by the same amount. Their relative probabilities do not move, although their absolute support has fallen.",
          "caption": 2,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Common shift preserves softmax but changes coverage",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/_runtime/matrix_decision.py",
              "lines": "391-404"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c2-beat-4",
          "claim": "Coverage looks at the strongest final score against a learned threshold. A sigmoid converts that margin into an estimate of answer availability.",
          "caption": 3,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Maximum-score sigmoid coverage",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/_runtime/matrix_decision.py",
              "lines": "399-403"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c2-beat-5",
          "claim": "With the lower scores, coverage falls from about eighty eight percent to twenty one percent. The relative preference still stays the same.",
          "caption": 4,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Illustrative arithmetic follows the coverage formula",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/_runtime/matrix_decision.py",
              "lines": "399-404"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c2-beat-6",
          "claim": "For an open choice, coverage scales every candidate probability. The remaining probability belongs to null: no acceptable candidate is present.",
          "caption": 5,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Joint null and candidate distribution",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/_runtime/matrix_decision.py",
              "lines": "399-410"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c2-beat-7",
          "claim": "Adding a weaker candidate leaves coverage unchanged when final scores are held fixed. Competition still redistributes probability among the candidates.",
          "caption": 6,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Fixed-score behavior",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/_runtime/matrix_decision.py",
              "lines": "391-404"
            },
            {
              "label": "Jeva paper v1, Section 3.3, Proposition 2",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2610.18773v1"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c2-beat-8",
          "claim": "That fixed score condition matters. In a full request, adding an option can change the refined state and therefore change the scores.",
          "caption": 7,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Set-dependent state precedes final scores",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/_runtime/matrix_decision.py",
              "lines": "366-388"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c2-beat-9",
          "claim": "A closed choice selects from the conditional probabilities. An open choice compares candidates and null, with null winning an exact tie.",
          "caption": 8,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Closed and open choice plus null tie",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/inference.py",
              "lines": "250-270"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c2-beat-10",
          "claim": "The Boolean interface returns the true candidate\u2019s probability. The score interface averages numeric rubric values using the conditional probabilities.",
          "caption": 9,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Typed interfaces",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/inference.py",
              "lines": "163-180"
            },
            {
              "label": "Noul and Score outputs",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/inference.py",
              "lines": "265-270"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c2-beat-11",
          "claim": "These are different readings of the same decision. Relative preference answers which option; coverage asks whether the set contains an answer.",
          "caption": 10,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Two separate probability calculations",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/_runtime/matrix_decision.py",
              "lines": "391-410"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "number": 3,
      "title": "Reuse the options, decide again",
      "claims": [
        {
          "id": "c3-beat-1",
          "claim": "The next request may ask a different question about the same options. Jeva can reuse their computation while making a fresh decision.",
          "caption": 0,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Fresh state and decision surrounding option-cache lookup",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/inference.py",
              "lines": "207-249"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c3-beat-2",
          "claim": "Start with an empty bank and three options. All three pass through the encoder, then their normalized vectors and layer projections are stored.",
          "caption": 1,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Cold hybrid option bank",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/inference.py",
              "lines": "224-241"
            },
            {
              "label": "Atomic option memories",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/_runtime/matrix_decision.py",
              "lines": "277-307"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c3-beat-3",
          "claim": "One cache keeps the assembled option tuple. The other keeps each option\u2019s vector, keys, and values, ready for a different combination.",
          "caption": 2,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Set and option caches",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/inference.py",
              "lines": "217-248"
            },
            {
              "label": "Memory data structures",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/_runtime/matrix_decision.py",
              "lines": "13-42"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c3-beat-4",
          "claim": "A new request with the same ordered tuple hits the set cache. The request still gets encoded, and the decision still runs.",
          "caption": 3,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Complete tuple hit with fresh state and decision",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/inference.py",
              "lines": "217-249"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c3-beat-5",
          "claim": "Now replace one option. The whole tuple misses, but hybrid caching finds the two retained options and encodes only the newcomer.",
          "caption": 4,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Partial option reuse on new tuple",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/inference.py",
              "lines": "224-241"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c3-beat-6",
          "claim": "A third combination uses three options already in the bank. Its tuple is new, yet all three option memories can be assembled without encoding them again.",
          "caption": 5,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "New tuple assembled from existing options",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/inference.py",
              "lines": "224-241"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c3-beat-7",
          "claim": "Cache identity follows tokenized, typed option text. Changing the weights or encoding policy invalidates the equivalence, so old memory must be cleared.",
          "caption": 6,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Token ID cache keys",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/inference.py",
              "lines": "211-218"
            },
            {
              "label": "Invalidation requirement",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "docs/runtime.md",
              "lines": "68-71"
            },
            {
              "label": "Jeva paper v1, Appendix B.5, cache identity",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2610.18773v1"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c3-beat-8",
          "claim": "The bank is bounded by entries and tensor bytes, with least recently used eviction. A miss pays the option encoding cost again.",
          "caption": 7,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Least recently used eviction and entry/byte caps",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/_runtime/cache.py",
              "lines": "12-30"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c3-beat-9",
          "claim": "More options still require more gathering, attention, and scoring. Bounded encoding batches keep all candidates, but do not remove individual text limits.",
          "caption": 8,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Bounded option encoding",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/inference.py",
              "lines": "129-142"
            },
            {
              "label": "Text length and memory limits",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "docs/runtime.md",
              "lines": "61-71"
            },
            {
              "label": "Jeva paper v1, Sections 3.4 and B.6",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2610.18773v1"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c3-beat-10",
          "claim": "Follow the reusable pieces: encode each option, retain its memory, assemble a set, and refine a fresh request. Shared memory never means a cached answer.",
          "caption": 9,
          "status": "verified",
          "sources": [
            {
              "label": "Reuse pipeline retains fresh per-request computation",
              "repo": "LiQiiiii/Jeva@f231c9ac31ba72f2ca4ca8aac662b279f3438fdb",
              "path": "src/jeva/inference.py",
              "lines": "220-249"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
